// Where every gandr program ends up: a computation returning a value.
//
// gandr is a CBPV (call-by-push-value) language. VALUES *are* -- numbers,
// strings, pairs, records -- and COMPUTATIONS *do* -- they run, perform
// effects, and (if all goes well) return a value. The two worlds never mix
// implicitly: a bare value is not a program, and `ret v` is the explicit
// bridge -- the computation that does nothing but return `v`.
//
// This program is written the way it is because it is the smallest honest
// gandr program: one computation item, one `ret`, one value.
/*
  Block comments are ordinary whitespace to the parser. They are useful when a
  note needs more room than a line comment, and they do not change the program:
  the last value is still the string returned just below.
*/
ret "hello, gandr"

// The harness checks:
//@ expect: clean
//@ expect-last-value: "hello, gandr"
